Can you provide a minimal repro?  The following simplest of examples *doesn't* trigger `unfulfilled-lint-expectations` on the [playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=nightly&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=ee144aaec3ea01644f2f7e59b1d6455d):


```rust
#![expect(missing_docs)]

pub struct Foo;
```

@rustbot label: -needs-triage +S-needs-repro +A-diagnostics +A-lints +D-incorrect +L-missing_docs

@eggyal Yes. Please see:

```rust
/// SQL keywords.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[non_exhaustive]
#[expect(missing_docs)]
pub enum Keyword {
    Null,
    CurrentTimestamp,
}
```

... where clippy doesn't complain.

Further minimized repro; the `derive` and the `cfg` are both necessary:

```rust
/// This enum has doc and its variants don’t
#[derive(Debug)]
#[expect(missing_docs)]
pub enum Value {
    Foo,
    #[cfg(false)]
    Bar,
}
```

I tried multiple toolchain versions and found that the problem reproduces with `cargo +nightly-2026-02-08 check` and not with `cargo +nightly-2026-02-07 check`.

I encountered what is probably this same issue myself with a very different reproduction:

```rust
// [dependencies]
// macro_rules_attribute = "0.2.2"

macro_rules! my_macro {
    ($($t:tt)*) => {};
}

/// Enum has doc
#[expect(missing_docs)]
#[derive(Clone)]
#[macro_rules_attribute::derive(my_macro!)]
pub enum Key {
    Foo, // no doc
}
```

In this case, the problem goes away (that is, `#[expect]` produces no diagnostic, as it should) if the order of the `#[derive]` and `#[macro_rules_attribute::derive]` attributes is swapped.

Types inside higher-ranked binders sometimes aren't checked for WF.

The following code errors

```rust
trait Trait: 'static {}

fn wut()
where
    for<'a> dyn Trait + 'a + Send:,
{
}

fn main() {
    wut();
}
```
```
error: higher-ranked lifetime error
  --> src/main.rs:10:5
   |
10 |     wut();
   |     ^^^^^
   |
   = note: could not prove `for<'a> (dyn Trait + Send + 'a) well-formed`
```

However, adding a `Send` bound makes it compile for some reason.

```rust
trait Trait: 'static {}

fn wut()
where
    for<'a> dyn Trait + 'a + Send: Send,
{
}

fn main() {
    wut();
}
```

This seems incorrect.

Also, here's a variant that makes rust produce an error mentioning #100013:

```rust
trait Trait<'b>: 'b {}

fn wut()
where
    for<'a, 'b> dyn Trait<'b> + 'a + Send:,
{
}

fn main() {
    wut();
}
```
```
error: lifetime bound not satisfied
  --> src/main.rs:10:5
   |
10 |     wut();
   |     ^^^^^
   |
note: the lifetime `'b` defined here...
  --> src/main.rs:5:13
   |
 5 |     for<'a, 'b> dyn Trait<'b> + 'a + Send:,
   |             ^^
note: ...must outlive the lifetime `'a` defined here
  --> src/main.rs:5:9
   |
 5 |     for<'a, 'b> dyn Trait<'b> + 'a + Send:,
   |         ^^
   = note: this is a known limitation that will be removed in the future (see issue #100013 <https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/100013> for more information)
```

Again, adding a `Send` bound makes it compile.

```rust
trait Trait<'b>: 'b {}

fn wut()
where
    for<'a, 'b> dyn Trait<'b> + 'a + Send: Send,
{
}

fn main() {
    wut();
}
```

