The `works1` and `works2` functions compile, but the `fails` function does not.

The `works1` function compiles. It moves from `y` into `x`, fails the guard, drops the `x`, then moves to the next arm. This makes sense.

The `fails` function errors. It matches against the left "branch" of the `_ | _` pattern, moves from `y` into `x`, fails the guard, then drops the `x`. It then matches against the right "branch" of the pattern, then attempts to move from `y` into `x` again, which isn't allowed. This makes sense.

The `works2` function compiles. I find this rather odd. It seems that the compiler does some extra reasoning only if the `if let` that moves is the last thing in the `if let` chain? I'm not sure. I can't tell if this is intended behavior or not.

