That's a funny case. The way [I specify or-patterns](https://nadrieril.github.io/rust-via-desugarings/pipeline/or-patterns.html), we should desugar these functions as follows:
```rust
fn works2() {
    let y = String::new();
    match () {
        _ if let x = y => {}
        _ if let x = y => {} // that's fine
        _ => {}
    }
}
fn fails() {
    let y = String::new();
    match () {
        _ if let x = y && false => {}
        _ if let x = y && false => {} // ERROR `y` is moved out of
        _ => {}
    }
}
```
These two compile the same in either form, so I'd argue this isn't a weirdness of or-patterns but of irrefutable if-let guards. I don't know what I think the right behavior should be, I feel conflicted.

cc @dianne

Comparing MIR dumps, there is indeed a false edge from the guard in `works2`, but it's before the guard's bindings are assigned, so it skips over the move:
```rust
bb5: {
    PlaceMention(_1); // y, i.e. _1, is the guard's scrutinee
    falseEdge -> [real: bb6, imaginary: bb2]; // try the next arm if `let _ = y` fails
}

bb6: {
    StorageLive(_3); // x, i.e. _3, is assigned after `let _ = y` succeeds
    _3 = move _1;
    _0 = const ();
    drop(_3) -> [return: bb7, unwind: bb12]; // end of match arm and function body
}
```

In `fails`, the first condition's bindings have to be assigned before evaluating the second guard condition (i.e. we assign `x = y` before evaluating `false`). Branching to the next arm after evaluating the second guard condition, then, is what gets us the double move:

```rust
bb5: {
    PlaceMention(_1); // y is the first guard condition's scrutinee
    falseEdge -> [real: bb6, imaginary: bb2]; // try the next arm if `let _ = y` fails
}

bb6: {
    StorageLive(_3); // x is assigned after `let _ = y` succeeds
    _3 = move _1;
    StorageLive(_4);
    _4 = const false;
    switchInt(move _4) -> [0: bb8, otherwise: bb7]; // try the next arm if `false` fails
}
```

So I don't think this has to do with irrefutable guards either. It's just that we evaluate bindings after matching, and we evaluate the next condition in a `let` chain after evaluating bindings. We get the same thing for refutable `if let` guards; the presence of a second guard condition operand makes it fail, even if that condition is an irrefutable `let`:

```rust
fn works2() {
    let y = Some(String::new());
    match () {
        _ if let Some(x) = y => {}
        _ if let Some(x) = y => {} // that's fine
        _ => {}
    }
}
fn fails() {
    let y = Some(String::new());
    match () {
        _ if let Some(x) = y && let _ = () => {}
        _ if let Some(x) = y && let _ = () => {} // ERROR `y` is moved out of
        _ => {}
    }
}
```

